        "eyebrow": "Virtual & luxury staging · New York City",
        "heading": "Luxury Staging NYC & Virtual Staging for Manhattan",
        "lede": "Virtual staging is a digital marketing technique that adds furniture and decor to real estate photographs: an interior designer furnishes empty apartments and cleared rooms in the image so buyers can see scale and layout, with nothing carried up the service elevator. Bella brings that approach to luxury staging in NYC — Manhattan condos, pre-war co-ops, penthouses and townhouses — with every photo staged in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo, the same price for a studio or a penthouse.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "Browse the lookbook",
        "alt_link": "/pages/lookbook",
        "city": "New York City",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the NYC market",
        "market_text": "<p>New York buyers make snap judgments on StreetEasy, and the city's housing stock makes empty photos brutal: pre-war co-ops with narrow rooms, new-development glass boxes that all look alike, and railroad layouts buyers can't mentally furnish. Home staging proves a queen bed fits in the second bedroom — the question that decides whether an NYC showing gets booked.</p><p>Physical staging in Manhattan and Brooklyn is a serious expense: furniture rental, a service elevator booking, a certificate of insurance for the building, and a crew who can work within board rules. Virtual staging skips all of it, which is why the cost comparison here is more lopsided than in any other US market.</p><p>Co-op and condo board logistics are the hidden cost people forget. A physical stage can stall for weeks waiting on building approval and elevator access; the photos can't wait that long when a listing's first week on StreetEasy determines most of its traffic.</p><p>New York staging companies that specialize in luxury work rent high-end furniture, art and accessories and install them room by room. Most home staging companies and staging firms in the city quote that work per project; it suits some trophy listings, but it typically means a larger upfront investment and longer lead times. The difference with virtual staging is speed and cost. We compare the city's physical stagers in our guide to <a href=\"/blogs/news/top-10-home-staging-new-york-companies\">luxury home staging companies in New York</a>; Bella is the virtual option for high-end listings that need to be online this week. See how it works on <a href=\"/pages/luxury-virtual-staging\">luxury virtual staging</a>.</p><p>97% of buyers use the internet in their home search (NAR), and New York buyers compare listing photos on StreetEasy long before a showing. Empty apartments photograph smaller and colder than they are, and staged photos tend to draw more clicks, views and showing requests. Virtual staging answers the scale question with furniture sized to the real room, so buyers can visualize how each space is used. Brokerages that need virtual staging New York buyers believe — including Brown Harris Stevens, a Bella client — use it on everything from pre-war co-ops to new developments.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Apartment staging NYC buyers can trust at the showing",
        "local_text": "<p>Our design team stages pre-war co-ops with furniture that respects the mouldings and proportions, new-development condos and penthouses with the clean contemporary look those buyers expect, and Brooklyn brownstones and townhouses somewhere between the two.</p><p>In home staging, NYC listings live or die on scale. Where the deciding question is usually whether real furniture fits, staging that quietly oversizes a room does more harm than good — we furnish to scale so the photo survives the showing.</p><p>For the photography, send wide, high-resolution photos in the best light you can get: daylight, lamps on, blinds open. The design team matches the lighting, textures and ceiling height in each photo so the furnishings sit realistically against the walls, and every final image is ready for the REBNY RLS and StreetEasy with disclosure guidance. Developers and teams marketing several apartments get volume discounts automatically — 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Upper East Side, Upper West Side, Tribeca, SoHo, West Village, Chelsea, Hudson Yards, Financial District, Brooklyn Heights, Park Slope, Williamsburg, Long Island City, Harlem, Astoria"
      }
